## 14. Enforcement (how these rules actually get followed)

Rules in **§5–§11** only help if reviewers and tooling **reach for them**.

| Lever | What to do |
|--------|------------|
| **PR review** | For any new or changed **framed mobile** screen: skim **§9** strip table (all roots present in **`rootPaddingReset`** / equivalent?), **§11** “Section header → Card” + Fragment/flex row, **§11** **Card — borderless section tiles** when **`customClass`** cards sit on a **page wash**, **§5.3** if **`TrailingIcons`** changed, **§5.4** if **`ListItem`** **`leading.avatar`** / logo images changed, and the **Layout check** paragraph at the end of **§11**. **`List` / `ListItem`:** confirm **§11** **List row dividers — no line on last row** — any **`.map`** that renders **`ListItem`** must pass **`separator={index < items.length - 1}`** (or equivalent); the component defaults **`separator={true}`**, which leaves a **bottom hairline** on the final row. |
| **Agents / Cursor** | Attach **`.cursor/design.mdc`** (or project rule that includes it) for layout and Mall-style screen work; cite **§11** + **`docs/layout-references/layout-pattern-catalog.md`** when picking **`LP-*`** patterns. Optional: set `alwaysApply: true` in this file’s front matter **only** if every task in the repo should weigh PODS shell rules — otherwise keep scoped to UI/layout work. |
| **Storybook as gate** | Ship a **story** that exercises the real shell path (**`scrollGutter`**, **`rootPaddingReset`**, stacked **SectionHeader** + **Card**). Visual review catches double gutters and oversized header–card gaps faster than grep. |
| **Automation (later)** | No shared linter enforces Fragment vs flex or **`ListItem`** separators yet. **Future:** optional **ESLint** (e.g. **`ListItem`** inside **`.map`** without a **`separator=`** prop; flex parents that only wrap fragment-wrapped section pairs with large **`gap`**) or **stylelint** heuristics — document any new rule next to this table. |

